<script>
const DATA={
"weeks":[
{"week":5,"title":"Week 5: Inflation","summary":"This week covers the definition of inflation, its causes, the means of addressing it through monetary and fiscal policy, and the reasons for variations in inflation rates across different countries.","sections":[["What is Inflation?","Inflation is the rate of increase in the prices of goods and services over time. It is a complex economic phenomenon that can have a significant impact on individuals, businesses, and the economy as a whole. High inflation can have a number of negative consequences: it can erode the purchasing power of consumers, reduce the profitability of businesses, and discourage investment. In severe cases, it can lead to economic instability and social unrest. <b>Recent Causes:</b> Supply chain disruptions, strong demand for goods and services, and the war in Ukraine."],["Addressing Inflation: Monetary Policy","<b>Inflation Targeting:</b> Many central banks operate under an inflation-targeting framework. They set a specific target for the inflation rate, often around 2%, and aim to keep inflation close to this target over the medium term. <b>Interest Rate Policy:</b> Central banks may raise the policy interest rate to cool down an overheating economy and reduce inflation. Higher interest rates make borrowing more expensive, leading to decreased spending and investment. <b>Open Market Operations:</b> Central banks conduct open market operations to buy or sell government securities. Selling securities reduces the money supply, raising interest rates and curbing inflation. Buying securities has the opposite effect. <b>Quantitative Tightening (QT):</b> Some central banks engage in quantitative tightening, which involves reducing the size of their balance sheet by selling assets. This process can help absorb excess liquidity from the financial system. <b>Forward Guidance:</b> Central banks provide forward guidance on their future policy intentions. Clear communication about the central bank's commitment to controlling inflation helps shape expectations in financial markets and among the public."],["Addressing Inflation: Fiscal & Supply-Side","<b>Collaboration with Fiscal Policy:</b> Coordination between monetary and fiscal policies can be effective in addressing inflation. Fiscal policies, such as taxation and government spending, can complement monetary efforts to achieve price stability. <b>Supply-Side Policies:</b> Policymakers may implement supply-side policies to address the root causes of inflation. Improving productivity, removing regulatory bottlenecks, and enhancing competition can help reduce cost-push inflation. <b>Exchange Rates:</b> In some cases, central banks may use exchange rate policies to influence inflation. A stronger domestic currency can lower import prices, contributing to lower inflation."],["Variation Across Countries","Inflation rates are different in various countries. There are specific reasons why this may be so. <b>Economic Conditions:</b> Countries experiencing robust economic growth may witness higher levels of demand for goods and services, potentially leading to inflationary pressures. <b>Monetary Policy:</b> The policies pursued by central banks play a crucial role in determining inflation. Differences in interest rates, money supply growth, and other monetary tools can lead to variations in inflation rates. <b>Fiscal Policy:</b> The fiscal policies of governments, including levels of government spending and taxation, can impact inflation. Excessive government spending may contribute to inflationary pressures. <b>Exchange Rates:</b> Countries with depreciating currencies may experience higher inflation, especially if they are net importers. A weaker currency can increase the cost of imported goods. <b>Supply and Demand Dynamics:</b> Supply shocks (natural disasters, geopolitical events) and demand changes can impact prices. <b>Demographic Factors:</b> Differences in population growth rates and age structure can impact consumption patterns and economic activity."]],"examTraps":["<b>Trap:</b> Confusing Inflation Targeting with Inflation Forecasting. Targeting is setting a goal (e.g., 2%); Forecasting is predicting future inflation to make informed decisions.","<b>Trap:</b> Thinking inflation is always bad. Moderate inflation can be a sign of a healthy economy, but high inflation is harmful. The lecture notes that inflation's benefits are one-sided and partial.","<b>Trap:</b> Forgetting that inflation varies across countries. The lecture explicitly states that inflation does NOT occur to the same degree everywhere, citing Nigeria, South Africa, the UK, and the US as examples.","<b>Trap:</b> Assuming all price increases are inflation. A one-off price increase (e.g., due to a temporary supply shock) is not the same as sustained inflation."],"takeaways":["Inflation = Rate of increase in prices.","Tools: Inflation targeting, interest rates, open market operations, quantitative tightening, forward guidance.","Fiscal policy: Taxation, government spending.","Supply-side policies: Improve productivity.","Variation reasons: Economic conditions, monetary policy, fiscal policy, exchange rates, supply/demand, demographics.","Inflation's benefits are partial and one-sided; it must be addressed for the general good."]},
{"week":6,"title":"Week 6: Unemployment","summary":"This week covers the definition of the labour force, how to compute the unemployment rate, the labour force-population ratio, and strategies for addressing unemployment.","sections":[["The Labour Force","The labour force (workforce) is the total number of individuals who are either employed or actively seeking employment in a particular country, region, or economic sector. It is a key concept in labour market analysis and is crucial for calculating various labour market indicators. <b>Employed Individuals:</b> Those who have jobs and are currently working, either part-time or full-time, for pay or profit. This includes individuals temporarily absent from work due to illness, vacation, or personal obligations. <b>Unemployed Individuals:</b> Those who do not have jobs but are actively seeking employment and are available for work. To be classified as unemployed, an individual must be actively engaged in job-seeking activities, such as submitting job applications, attending job interviews, or registering with employment agencies."],["Computing the Unemployment Rate","<b>Labour Force</b> = Number of employed + Number of unemployed. <b>Labour Force-Population Ratio</b> = (Labour Force / Working-Age Population) × 100. It is a useful metric for assessing the level of economic activity and gauging the utilization of the available workforce. If the ratio is 20%, it means that 20% of the working-age population are in the labour force. <b>Unemployment Rate</b> = (Number of Unemployed / Labour Force) × 100%. <b>Example:</b> Suppose a country has a labour force of 150 million, with 10 million individuals unemployed. Unemployment rate = (10 million / 150 million) × 100% = 6.67%."],["Addressing Unemployment","<b>1. Invest in Education and Skill Development:</b> Prioritise education and skill development programs to equip individuals with the skills needed in evolving industries. <b>2. Promote Entrepreneurship:</b> Create an environment that encourages entrepreneurship by offering support, financial incentives, and resources for individuals to start their own businesses. <b>3. Labour Market Flexibility:</b> Establish policies that promote labor market flexibility, allowing businesses to adapt to changing economic conditions. <b>4. Government Policies and Stimulus:</b> Implement fiscal and monetary policies aimed at stimulating economic growth. <b>5. Workforce Training and Reskilling:</b> Develop training and reskilling programs to enhance the adaptability of the workforce. <b>6. Community Development Initiatives:</b> Implement community development initiatives that address specific regional challenges. <b>7. Monitoring and Evaluation:</b> Regularly monitor and evaluate the effectiveness of unemployment reduction strategies. <b>8. Social Safety Nets:</b> Establish social safety nets to provide financial support and assistance to individuals facing unemployment."]],"examTraps":["<b>Trap:</b> Including discouraged workers in the labour force. Discouraged workers (those who have given up looking) are not counted in official figures, which can impact the accuracy of labor market indicators.","<b>Trap:</b> Confusing the Labour Force-Population Ratio with the Unemployment Rate. The ratio measures the proportion of the working-age population in the labour force; the rate measures the proportion of the labour force that is unemployed.","<b>Trap:</b> Thinking unemployment is only about lack of jobs. It also includes underemployment (working part-time but wanting full-time work) and skills mismatch.","<b>Trap:</b> Forgetting that retirees, students, and individuals with disabilities may not be part of the labour force if they are not actively seeking employment."],"takeaways":["Labour Force = Employed + Unemployed.","Unemployment Rate = (Unemployed / Labour Force) × 100.","Labour Force-Population Ratio = (Labour Force / Working-Age Population) × 100.","Addressing unemployment: Education, entrepreneurship, flexibility, stimulus, training, community development, monitoring, social safety nets.","Discouraged workers are not counted in official figures."]},
{"week":7,"title":"Week 7: Taxation","summary":"This week covers the types of taxation (Regressive, Progressive, Proportional), the various forms of taxes, and the factors responsible for variations in tax income across countries.","sections":[["Types of Taxation","<b>Regressive Tax:</b> This type of tax once administered affects low-income earners much more than their richer counterparts. This is because it has a decreasing tax rate as the tax base increases, thus an inverse relationship existing between the tax rate and the taxpayer's ability to pay. Examples: Poll Tax, Lump-Sum Tax, Pigovian Taxes. <b>Progressive Tax:</b> This type of tax is administered such that taxpayers pay based on their respective abilities. The tax burden is lower for low-income earners and higher for their richer counterparts. It follows that as one's income rises one pays a higher tax. Examples: Capital Gains Tax, Luxury Sales Tax, Estate Tax. <b>Proportional Tax:</b> This type of tax indulges a uniform tax rate for taxpayers. Though it may seem low-income earners are paying lower than high-income earners, what matters is that both income groups enjoy the same tax rate. The burden is more on the poor than the rich since a higher fraction of income from the poor is spent on consumables compared to the rich (the poor have a higher marginal propensity to consume)."],["Forms of Taxes","<b>Income Tax:</b> Taxes on financial income earned by labour. <b>Corporate Tax:</b> Taxes on financial income earned by business units. <b>Capital Gains Tax:</b> Taxes imposed on profits from financial or physical assets. <b>Property Tax:</b> Taxes imposed on the value of properties. <b>Inheritance Tax:</b> Taxes imposed on bequeathed properties or valuables. <b>Sales Tax:</b> A consumption tax imposed on the sale of goods and/or services. It is typically collected at the point of sale and passed on to the government by the seller. <b>Value-Added Tax (VAT):</b> A tax on the value added to goods and services at each stage of the production and distribution process. It is a common type of tax in Europe and other parts of the world, including Nigeria."],["Variation Across Countries","Various economies impose taxes to the best of their capabilities. The difference, however, in the tax income generated by these countries depend on a myriad of factors, some of which include: <b>The Tax Base</b> (the national income level), <b>the efficiency of tax administration</b>, <b>incidence of tax evasion</b>, <b>potency of the judiciary structure</b> in penalising evaders, <b>tax compliance policies</b>, and so on. Even if economies enjoy same national income levels, there would still be variations in tax income due to the unobserved heterogeneity of each country."]],"examTraps":["<b>Trap:</b> Thinking Regressive and Proportional are the same. Regressive has a decreasing rate; Proportional has a constant rate. Both place a heavier burden on the poor.","<b>Trap:</b> Confusing Progressive with Proportional. Progressive increases with income; Proportional is constant.","<b>Trap:</b> Forgetting that a Proportional tax places a heavier burden on the poor because they spend a higher fraction of their income on consumables (higher marginal propensity to consume).","<b>Trap:</b> Assuming higher tax revenue means a better tax system. As the lecture notes, variations can be due to factors like tax evasion, not just tax rates."],"takeaways":["Regressive: Higher burden on poor, rate decreases.","Progressive: Higher burden on rich, rate increases.","Proportional: Constant rate, burden heavier on poor.","Forms: Income, Corporate, Capital Gains, Property, Inheritance, Sales, VAT.","Variations: Tax base, administration, evasion, judiciary, compliance."]},
{"week":8,"title":"Week 8: The Circular Flow Diagram","summary":"This week introduces the circular flow of income, a primary model that explains how income is generated, distributed, and redistributed in an economy. It focuses on the two-sector economy consisting of households and business units.","sections":[["Definition","The circular flow of income is a primary model which explains how income is first generated, distributed and redistributed in a given economy within a period of time. It treats income as a flow and explains how one economic agent generates it and later transfers it to another. The flow diagram as the name implies treats income as it is commonly known - a flow - and explains how one economic agent generates it and later transfers it to another."],["Two-Sector Economy","A two-sector economy is one in which there are only two economic agents. This is one of the simplest forms in which an economy can exist. The two agents involved here are the <b>Households</b> and the <b>Business Units</b>. <b>Household:</b> Represents a group of individuals who share living accommodation and resources for survival within a given period of time. <b>Business Unit:</b> An agent saddled with the responsibility of harnessing factors of production for the continued production of finished goods and services."],["Assumptions of the Model","For the circular flow model to work, certain assumptions must hold: <b>1.</b> The Household does not invest. <b>2.</b> The business does not consume. <b>3.</b> Households consume. <b>4.</b> Business units invest. <b>5.</b> Households own the factor inputs. <b>6.</b> The business units demand factor inputs and pay a price. <b>7.</b> There are no leakages or withdrawals (money generated as income does not leave the economy). <b>8.</b> There are no injections (money not created as income within the economy by her agents is not infused into the economy). Assumptions 7 and 8 are necessary to avoid incidence of distortion in market equilibrium."],["The Flow Diagram","<b>Firms</b> produce finished goods and services using factor inputs owned by the households – land, labour, capital and the entrepreneur. <b>Households</b> consume the finished goods and services produced by the firms but then have earned income from offering their factor inputs to the firms. The exchange of money for finished goods and services is done in the <b>commodity market</b>. The transaction between the household and firms where money is exchanged for the purchase of finished goods and services is conducted in the <b>factor market</b>. <b>Real vs Money Flows:</b> The blue loop represents the flow of real commodities (finished goods and services) from the firms to the households and the flow of factors of production (inputs) from the households to the firms. The brown loop represents the flow of money from the households to the firms for the purchase of finished goods and services and the flow of money from the firms to the households for the purchase of factors of production."]],"examTraps":["<b>Trap:</b> Confusing the Commodity Market with the Factor Market. Commodity market = goods and services. Factor market = factors of production (land, labour, capital).","<b>Trap:</b> Forgetting that in a two-sector economy, there is no government and no international trade. Taxes and imports/exports are not included.","<b>Trap:</b> Assuming households and firms are the same. Households consume and own factors; Firms produce and invest.","<b>Trap:</b> Thinking leakages and injections are the same. Leakages (savings) remove money from the flow; injections (investment) add money to the flow."],"takeaways":["Circular Flow = Income generation, distribution, and redistribution.","Two-Sector Economy: Households and Business Units.","Assumptions: No leakages, no injections, households own factors.","Commodity Market: Goods and services. Factor Market: Factors of production.","Real flow (blue): Goods and factors. Money flow (brown): Payments."]}
],
"questions":[
{"week":"5","q":"What is inflation?","opts":["The rate of increase in the prices of goods and services over time","The rate of decrease in prices","The rate of unemployment","The rate of taxation"],"a":0,"why":"Inflation is the rate of increase in the prices of goods and services over time."},
{"week":"5","q":"What is inflation targeting?","opts":["Setting a specific target for the inflation rate","Ignoring inflation","Increasing inflation","Reducing unemployment"],"a":0,"why":"Many central banks operate under an inflation-targeting framework, setting a specific target (often around 2%)."},
{"week":"5","q":"What is the purpose of raising the policy interest rate?","opts":["To cool down an overheating economy and reduce inflation","To increase inflation","To increase unemployment","To decrease taxes"],"a":0,"why":"Central banks may raise the policy interest rate to cool down an overheating economy and reduce inflation."},
{"week":"5","q":"What is quantitative tightening (QT)?","opts":["Reducing the size of the central bank's balance sheet by selling assets","Increasing the money supply","Lowering interest rates","Increasing government spending"],"a":0,"why":"Quantitative tightening involves reducing the size of the central bank's balance sheet by selling assets to absorb excess liquidity."},
{"week":"5","q":"What is forward guidance?","opts":["Providing information on future policy intentions","Increasing interest rates","Reducing money supply","Increasing taxes"],"a":0,"why":"Forward guidance is providing information on the central bank's future policy intentions to shape expectations."},
{"week":"5","q":"Why do inflation rates vary across countries?","opts":["Economic conditions","Monetary policy","Fiscal policy","All of the above"],"a":3,"why":"Inflation rates vary due to economic conditions, monetary policy, fiscal policy, exchange rates, supply and demand dynamics, and demographic factors."},
{"week":"5","q":"Which of the following is a cause of inflation?","opts":["Supply chain disruptions","Strong demand","War","All of the above"],"a":3,"why":"Inflation can be caused by supply chain disruptions, strong demand, war, and other factors."},
{"week":"5","q":"What is the effect of high inflation on purchasing power?","opts":["It erodes purchasing power","It increases purchasing power","It has no effect","It stabilizes purchasing power"],"a":0,"why":"High inflation erodes the purchasing power of consumers."},
{"week":"5","q":"What is the purpose of supply-side policies in addressing inflation?","opts":["To address the root causes of inflation","To increase demand","To increase government spending","To increase taxes"],"a":0,"why":"Supply-side policies aim to address the root causes of inflation by improving productivity and enhancing competition."},
{"week":"5","q":"How can exchange rate policies influence inflation?","opts":["A stronger domestic currency can lower import prices","A weaker domestic currency can lower import prices","Exchange rates have no effect","Exchange rates only affect exports"],"a":0,"why":"A stronger domestic currency can lower import prices, contributing to lower inflation."},
{"week":"6","q":"What is the labour force?","opts":["The total number of individuals who are either employed or actively seeking employment","The total number of employed individuals","The total number of unemployed individuals","The total population"],"a":0,"why":"The labour force is the total number of individuals who are either employed or actively seeking employment."},
{"week":"6","q":"How is the labour force calculated?","opts":["Number of employed + Number of unemployed","Number of employed - Number of unemployed","Number of unemployed / Labour force","Total population / Working-age population"],"a":0,"why":"Labour Force = Number of employed + Number of unemployed."},
{"week":"6","q":"How is the unemployment rate calculated?","opts":["(Number of Unemployed / Labour Force) × 100%","(Labour Force / Working-Age Population) × 100","(Number of Employed / Labour Force) × 100%","(Total Population / Labour Force) × 100"],"a":0,"why":"Unemployment rate = (Number of Unemployed / Labour Force) × 100%."},
{"week":"6","q":"If a country has a labour force of 150 million and 10 million unemployed, what is the unemployment rate?","opts":["6.67%","10%","15%","5%"],"a":0,"why":"Unemployment rate = (10/150) × 100 = 6.67%."},
{"week":"6","q":"Which of the following is a strategy for addressing unemployment?","opts":["Invest in education and skill development","Promote entrepreneurship","Labour market flexibility","All of the above"],"a":3,"why":"All of these are strategies for addressing unemployment."},
{"week":"6","q":"What is the labour force-population ratio?","opts":["(Labour Force / Working-Age Population) × 100","(Number of Unemployed / Labour Force) × 100","(Number of Employed / Labour Force) × 100","(Total Population / Labour Force) × 100"],"a":0,"why":"Labour Force-Population Ratio = (Labour Force / Working-Age Population) × 100."},
{"week":"6","q":"Which of the following is NOT a strategy for addressing unemployment?","opts":["Reducing education and skill development","Promoting entrepreneurship","Labour market flexibility","Government policies and stimulus"],"a":0,"why":"Reducing education and skill development would worsen unemployment, not address it."},
{"week":"6","q":"What is a discouraged worker?","opts":["Someone who has given up on finding employment","Someone who is employed","Someone who is actively seeking employment","Someone who is retired"],"a":0,"why":"Discouraged workers have given up on finding employment and may not be counted in official labour force figures."},
{"week":"6","q":"Why is monitoring and evaluation important in addressing unemployment?","opts":["To ensure interventions are responsive to changing economic conditions","To increase taxes","To reduce education","To increase unemployment"],"a":0,"why":"Monitoring and evaluation help adjust policies based on data-driven insights."},
{"week":"6","q":"What is the relationship between the labour force and the working-age population?","opts":["The labour force is a subset of the working-age population","The working-age population is a subset of the labour force","They are the same","There is no relationship"],"a":0,"why":"The labour force is a subset of the working-age population (it excludes those not actively seeking employment)."},
{"week":"7","q":"What is a regressive tax?","opts":["A tax that affects low-income earners much more than the rich","A tax that affects high-income earners more than the poor","A tax with a uniform rate","A tax on luxury goods"],"a":0,"why":"A regressive tax affects low-income earners much more than their richer counterparts."},
{"week":"7","q":"What is a progressive tax?","opts":["A tax where the rate increases as income increases","A tax where the rate decreases as income increases","A tax with a uniform rate","A tax on consumption"],"a":0,"why":"A progressive tax is administered such that taxpayers pay based on their respective abilities, with higher rates for higher incomes."},
{"week":"7","q":"What is a proportional tax?","opts":["A tax with a uniform tax rate for all taxpayers","A tax that increases with income","A tax that decreases with income","A tax on property only"],"a":0,"why":"A proportional tax indulges a uniform tax rate for taxpayers."},
{"week":"7","q":"Which of the following is an example of a regressive tax?","opts":["Poll Tax","Capital Gains Tax","Luxury Sales Tax","Estate Tax"],"a":0,"why":"Poll Tax, Lump-Sum Tax, and Pigovian Taxes are examples of regressive taxes."},
{"week":"7","q":"Which of the following is an example of a progressive tax?","opts":["Capital Gains Tax","Poll Tax","Lump-Sum Tax","Pigovian Tax"],"a":0,"why":"Capital Gains Tax, Luxury Sales Tax, and Estate Tax are examples of progressive taxes."},
{"week":"7","q":"Why does a proportional tax place a heavier burden on the poor?","opts":["Because the poor have a higher marginal propensity to consume","Because the poor have a lower marginal propensity to consume","Because the rich pay a higher rate","Because the rich have more assets"],"a":0,"why":"The poor have a higher marginal propensity to consume, so a uniform tax rate takes a larger fraction of their income."},
{"week":"7","q":"What is VAT?","opts":["A tax on the value added to goods and services at each stage of production and distribution","A tax on income","A tax on property","A tax on imports"],"a":0,"why":"VAT is a tax on the value added to goods and services at each stage of production and distribution."},
{"week":"7","q":"What is the tax base?","opts":["The income on which a tax is incident","The total tax revenue","The tax rate","The government spending"],"a":0,"why":"A tax base is the income on which a tax is incident."},
{"week":"7","q":"Why do tax incomes vary across countries?","opts":["Differences in tax base, administration efficiency, and evasion","Differences in population only","Differences in weather","Differences in language"],"a":0,"why":"Tax income varies due to tax base, efficiency of tax administration, incidence of tax evasion, judiciary potency, and compliance policies."},
{"week":"7","q":"What is the tax revenue as a ratio of GDP?","opts":["The total tax revenue divided by GDP","The tax rate divided by GDP","The tax base divided by GDP","The government spending divided by GDP"],"a":0,"why":"Tax revenue as a ratio of GDP is a measure of the tax burden in an economy."},
{"week":"8","q":"What is the circular flow of income?","opts":["A model that explains how income is generated, distributed, and redistributed","A model of government spending","A model of international trade","A model of consumption only"],"a":0,"why":"The circular flow of income is a primary model which explains how income is first generated, distributed and redistributed in a given economy within a period of time."},
{"week":"8","q":"Which two economic agents are involved in a two-sector economy?","opts":["Government and households","Households and business units","Government and business units","International traders and households"],"a":1,"why":"A two-sector economy consists of households and business units."},
{"week":"8","q":"What is a household?","opts":["A group of individuals who share living accommodation and resources for survival","A business unit","A government agency","An international trader"],"a":0,"why":"A household represents a group of individuals who share living accommodation and resources for survival within a given period of time."},
{"week":"8","q":"What is a business unit?","opts":["An agent saddled with the responsibility of harnessing factors of production","A group of consumers","A government agency","An international trader"],"a":0,"why":"A business unit is an agent saddled with the responsibility of harnessing factors of production for the continued production of finished goods and services."},
